{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791630613,"nonce":"gBKs34u/LmqLakytkf9hq6RFm+M=","title":"The SSA builder's own tables still hash with SipHash","message":"After f49adcb the SsaBuilder kept std HashSet/HashMap for its address-taken and let-bound names and its shared undefs, the iterated-dominance-frontier sets (def blocks per variable, compute_idf's worklist sets), the CFG-order maps of the loop pre-pass, trivial-phi maps and apply_substitutions' map, which is read for every operand of every instruction. Sampling Python nbody start-up showed RandomState inserts, InternedString hashing and replace_uses over a RandomState map inside build_from_typed_cfg.\n\nFix: those builder-internal tables are Fx (IdHashMap/IdHashSet/IdMap). The tables the builder shares with LoweringContext (symbols, fiber and body function names, resume parameter names, the typed CFG's loop map) keep their types. Phi placement iterates the IDF in Fx order, so value numbering differs while the placed phis are the same.\n\nStart-up instructions over afcb8613 (llvm-backend build, PROFILE_STARTUP=10, ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1 so background compiles do not vary run to run; the static-hot compile still runs, on the calling thread), median of 3, Mac: 17 Lua benchmarks 13.719G -\u003e 13.457G (-1.9%; every benchmark lower, nbody -3.1%, pipeline -2.6%, method_call -2.4%); 8 Python kernels 38.98G -\u003e 38.35G (-1.6%; scimark_fft -4.3%), outputs unchanged. Optimised bodies identical after id masking on all 17 Lua benchmarks and 7 Python kernels.","files":null}]}